Deploy a Lambda Function Locally
Learning Objectives
Deploy and manage LocalStack as a Lambda emulation environment using Docker
Design a Python Lambda handler conforming to the AWS Lambda execution contract
Package, deploy, and version Lambda functions using AWS CLI against a local endpoint
Perform function updates and validate behavioral changes through synchronous invocation
Analyze invocation payloads, logs, and response structures for debugging serverless workloads
Prerequisites
Solid understanding of AWS Lambda concepts (handlers, event/context objects, IAM execution roles)
Working knowledge of Docker, Docker Compose, and container networking
Proficiency with AWS CLI configuration and command structure
Python 3.x scripting experience
Familiarity with JSON and IAM trust policy documents
Environment Setup
Al Nafi provides a single Linux machine (Start Lab) with sudo access
Verify/install dependencies:
docker --version
docker compose version
python3 --version
aws --version
pip3 install awscli-local
If any tool is missing, install via your distro's package manager (apt, yum) or pip
Confirm Docker daemon is active: sudo systemctl status docker
Architecture Overview
[Host Machine]
   |
   +-- Docker Engine (socket mounted into LocalStack container)
   |
   +-- LocalStack Container
   |      - Lambda service (executes handler in sub-containers)
   |      - IAM service (execution role validation)
   |
   +-- AWS CLI (--endpoint-url=http://localhost:4566)
   |
   +-- Local filesystem: handler.py -> function.zip
Design decisions you must make:

Lambda executor mode (Docker vs Docker-reuse) — evaluate trade-offs for cold-start latency
IAM role strategy — LocalStack accepts dummy ARNs, but structure them realistically
Payload structure for input/output contract between invoker and handler
